WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.080
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.240
This paper is called Enhancing photorealism
enhancement.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.240
Hmm!

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.190
Let’s try to unpack what that exactly means.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:20.740
This means that we take video footage from
a game, for instance, GTA 5, which is an action

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:26.330
game where the city we can play in was modeled
after real places in California.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:31.730
Now, as we are living the advent of neural
network-based learning algorithms, we have

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.200
a ton of training data at our disposal on
the internet.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:42.180
For instance, the cityscapes dataset contains
images and videos taken in 50 real cities,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.110
and it also contains annotations that describe
which object is which.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:53.040
And the authors of this paper looked at this,
and had an absolutely insane idea.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:59.620
And the idea is let’s learn on the cityscapes
dataset what cars, cities and architecture

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:06.720
looks like, then take a piece of video footage
from the game, and translate it into a real

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.820
movie.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.600
So basically something that is impossible.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.330
That is an insane idea, and when I read this
paper, I thought that cannot possibly work

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:24.580
in any case, but especially not given that
the game takes place in California, and the

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.170
Cityscapes dataset contains mostly footage
of German cities.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.830
How would a learning algorithm pull that off?

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.490
There is no way this will work.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:39.689
Now, there are previous techniques that attempted
this, here you see a few of them.

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:46.180
And…well, the realism is just not there,
and there was an even bigger issue.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.369
And that is the lack of temporal coherence.

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:54.750
This is the flickering that you see where
the AI processes these images independently

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.740
and does not do that consistently.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.009
This quickly breaks the immersion and is typically
a deal-breaker.

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:07.340
And now, hold on to your papers…and let’s
have a look together at the new technique.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.619
Whoa!

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:12.610
This is nothing like the previous ones!

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:18.880
It renders the exact same place, the exact
same cars, and the badges are still correct

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.110
and still refer to real-world brands.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.319
And that’s not even the best part, look!

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:31.050
The carpaint materials are significantly more
realistic, something that is really difficult

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.110
to capture in a real-time rendering engine.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.750
Lots of realistic looking specular highlights
off of something that feels like the real

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.920
geometry of the car.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:42.930
Wow.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:49.990
Now, as you see, most of the generated photorealistic
images are dimmer, and less saturated than

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.990
the video game graphics.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.290
Why is that?

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:59.760
This is because computer game engines often
create a more stylized world where the saturation,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.800
haze, and bloom effects are often more pronounced.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:09.230
Let’s try to fight this bias where many
people consider the more saturated images

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:14.920
to be better, and focus our attention to the
realism in these image pairs.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:21.180
While we are there, for reference, we can
have a look at what the output would be if

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:26.880
we didn’t do any of the photorealistic magic,
but instead, we just tried to breathe more

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.709
life into the video game footage by trying
to transfer the color schemes from these real-world

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:35.060
videos in the training set.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.900
So, only color transfer.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.569
Let’s see.

00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:45.890
Yes, that helps…until we compare the results
with the photorealistic images synthesized

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.620
by this new AI.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:48.690
Look.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:54.590
The trees don’t look nearly as realistic
as the new method, and after we see the real

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:58.730
roads, it’s hard to settle for the synthetic
ones from the game.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:04.710
However, no one said that Cityscapes is the
only dataset we can use for this method.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:10.440
In fact, if we still find ourselves yearning
for that saturated look, we can try to plug

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.239
in a more stylized dataset, and get…this!

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:20.949
This is fantastic, because these images don’t
have many of the limitations of computer graphics

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:22.879
rendering systems.

00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:24.410
Why is that?

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.840
Because, look at the grass here.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:32.279
In the game, it looks like a 2D texture to
save resources and be able to render an image

00:04:32.279 --> 00:04:33.319
quicker.

00:04:33.319 --> 00:04:40.449
However, the new system can put more real-looking
grass in there, which is a fully 3D object

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:43.849
where every single blade of grass is considered.

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:49.830
The most mind-blowing thing here is that this
AI finally has enough generalization capabilities

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:55.889
to learn about cities in Germany, and still
be able to make convincing photorealistic

00:04:55.889 --> 00:04:57.460
images for California.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:04.349
The algorithm never saw California, and yet,
it can recreate it from video game footage

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:07.649
better than I ever imagined would be possible.

00:05:07.649 --> 00:05:10.620
That is mind blowing.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.620
Unreal.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:16.589
And if you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper.

00:05:16.589 --> 00:05:22.189
Because here, we have one of those rare cases
where we squeeze our papers for not a feature,

00:05:22.189 --> 00:05:25.169
but for a limitation…of sorts.

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:27.930
You see, there are limits to this technique
too.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:34.189
For instance since the AI was trained on the
beautiful lush hills of Germany and Austria,

00:05:34.189 --> 00:05:37.580
it hasn’t really seen the dry hills of LA.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.439
So, what does it do with them?

00:05:41.439 --> 00:05:48.409
Look, it redrew the hills the only way it
saw hills exist, which is, with trees.

00:05:48.409 --> 00:05:55.120
Now, we can think of this as a limitation,
but also as an opportunity.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.900
Just imagine the amazing artistic effects
we could achieve by playing this trick to

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.240
our advantage.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:07.740
Also, we won’t need to create an 80% photorealistic
game like this one and push it up to a 100%

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.059
with the AI.

00:06:09.059 --> 00:06:17.110
We could draw not 80%, but the bare minimum,
maybe only 20% for the video game, a coarse

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:22.059
draft, if you will, and let the AI do the
heavy lifting!

00:06:22.059 --> 00:06:26.469
Imagine how much modeling time we could save
for artists as well.

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:28.610
I love this.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:29.969
What a time to be alive!

00:06:29.969 --> 00:06:36.270
Now, all of this only makes sense for real-world
use if it can run quickly.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.189
So can it?

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:41.849
How long do we have to wait to get such a
photorealistic video?

00:06:41.849 --> 00:06:45.110
Do we wait from minutes to hours?

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:46.169
No!

00:06:46.169 --> 00:06:51.590
The whole thing runs interactively, which
means that it is already usable, we can plug

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.990
this into the game as a post-processing step.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:59.919
And remember the First Law Of Papers, which
says that two more papers down the line, and

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:02.369
it will be even better.

00:07:02.369 --> 00:07:05.369
What improvements do you expect to happen
soon?

00:07:05.369 --> 00:07:07.900
And what would you use this for?

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:53.679
Let me know in the comments below!

00:07:53.679 --> 00:08:02.169
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

